  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nTonight-Friday: Heavy Rainfall \n\nThe longwave trough situated to our east will shift off to the east \nthis afternoon. Shortwave ridging moves into the Upper Midwest for \nthe day and at the same time, a shortwave trough begins to develop \nacross the northern Plains. As this trough shifts southeastward, it \nwill deepen. At the surface, a low forms this evening and progresses \nsoutheastward through the overnight tonight and through the day on \nFriday. With southwest flow advecting in PWATs between 1.7 and 2.2\", \nwhich corresponds to the 90 to 97.5 percentiles based on the \nNAEFS, combining with the good moisture convergence, there will \nbe ample lift and moisture for a heavy rain event to unfold. The\ngood news is that due to our position to the north of the low \nand the timing of when the low begins to impact our CWA, limited\nto no instability is expected to build in. Further south into \ncentral Iowa, they will have a break in between the first wave \nof rain and when scattered storms develop. A low tornado risk is\npossible down there due to the very low LCLs around 1000ft or \nless and the amount of low-level shear (around 20 to 30 kts of \n0-1km shear) and if enough clearing happens. The only way any \npart of our CWA sees a low tornado risk is if the track of the \nlow shifts further north, allowing portions of the area, \nprobably northeast Iowa, to have that low tornado potential \n(<5%) due to the previously mentioned conditions in central \nIowa. \n\nFocusing on the heavy rain potential, EFI values have a swath of 0.7 \nto 0.85 across southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa and into far \nsouthwest Wisconsin indicating that this has the potential to be on \nthe higher side of climatological normal. There is also a SOT up to \n1.5 for northeast Iowa, which indicates some guidance is showing \namounts up to 5\". The probabilities for >1\" in the GEFS have \ncontinue to increase over the past couple of days with values of 60 \nto 70% in southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa. Meanwhile the EPS \nhas a 80 to 100% chance for the same areas. There are differences in \nthe exact track of the low which will influence where the highest \ntotals end up. Another point to mention is the warm cloud depth \nlayer, around 4000m, that will help increase rain amounts. Model \nguidance continues to show an initial wave of showers and maybe some \nstorms move through the area during the overnight and early morning \nFriday, followed by either continued stratiform rain (for areas \nnorth of the low) or more scattered showers/storms (for areas near \nand south of the low). In general, 1 to 2.5\" is expected across \nsoutheast Minnesota, northeast Iowa, and far southwest Wisconsin. \nAreas north of I-90 in Wisconsin are expected to receive between \n0.25 to 1\". Locally higher amounts are possible, potentially up to \n5\" for portions of southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa. These \nexpected amounts remain uncertain due to the exact track of the low \nand the fact that there is little to no instability in place to help \nboost rain rates. At the same time there is the deep warm cloud \nlayer that would boost rain amounts. Overall heavy rain is expected \nacross portions of the Upper Midwest, favored in southeast Minnesota \nand northeast Iowa.  \n\nSaturday-Thursday: Seasonable Temperatures with Dry Conditions \nEarly, Rain Chances Later in the Week\n\nRidging builds back in through early next week keeping the region \ndry into Tuesday. Ensemble and deterministic guidance both support a \ntrough impacting the Upper Midwest for the latter portions of next \nweek. This would increase shower and storm chances starting Tuesday \n(25 to 45%). There are uncertainties with when this trough moves \nthrough the region, for instance the GFS is now slower than the \nECMWF in bringing the next bout of rain. It is now more on Wednesday \nthan Tuesday. Temperatures across the forecast period remain \nseasonable with highs largely in the upper 70s to mid 80s. The lone \nexception is Friday where highs will only get into the upper 60s to \nmid 70s.",
